Yes. A **Functional Interface** **can extend another interface**, but there is **one strict rule**.

**Restriction:**
After inheritance, the interface must still have **exactly one abstract method**.

---

**Case 1 — Valid (still one abstract method)**

```java
interface A {
    void run();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A {
}
```

Usage

```java
B obj = () -> System.out.println("Hello");
obj.run();
```

✔ Valid because total abstract methods = **1**

---

**Case 2 — Invalid (two abstract methods)**

```java
interface A {
    void run();
}

interface C {
    void stop();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A, C { }
```

❌ Compile error

Reason

```text
Multiple abstract methods found
```

---

**Case 3 — Valid (default methods allowed)**

```java
interface A {
    void run();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A {

    default void log(){
        System.out.println("log");
    }
}
```

✔ Allowed because **default methods don't count**

---

**Case 4 — Valid (methods from Object allowed)**

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Test {

    boolean equals(Object obj);

    void execute();
}
```

✔ Allowed because methods from **Object class** don't count.

---

**Interview One-liner**

```text
A functional interface can extend other interfaces, but after inheritance it must still contain exactly one abstract method.
```

---

**Real Example from Java**

```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface UnaryOperator<T>
        extends java.util.function.Function<T,T> {
}
```

Here **UnaryOperator** extends **Function**, yet remains functional.

