### **✅ Best Practices for Creating an ETL Utility with Node.js**

When building an **ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) utility** in **Node.js**, follow these best practices:

---

## **1️⃣ Choose the Right Libraries**

### **Recommended 3rd-Party Libraries**

| Purpose                   | Library                                                  | Description                                                   |
| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Batch Processing**      | [`bull`](https://github.com/OptimalBits/bull)            | Redis-backed job queue for handling large data efficiently    |
|                           | [`agenda`](https://github.com/agenda/agenda)             | MongoDB-based job scheduling for cron-like ETL tasks          |
| **Streaming & Pipelines** | [`stream`](https://nodejs.org/api/stream.html)           | Node.js native stream API for handling large data efficiently |
|                           | [`fast-csv`](https://github.com/C2FO/fast-csv)           | High-performance CSV parser/writer                            |
| **Database Handling**     | [`knex`](https://knexjs.org/)                            | Query builder for SQL databases                               |
|                           | [`mongoose`](https://mongoosejs.com/)                    | ODM for MongoDB                                               |
| **API Requests**          | [`axios`](https://axios-http.com/)                       | HTTP client for external data extraction                      |
|                           | [`node-fetch`](https://github.com/node-fetch/node-fetch) | Lightweight HTTP client for making API requests               |
| **Logging & Monitoring**  | [`winston`](https://github.com/winstonjs/winston)        | Logging with file/console transports                          |
|                           | [`pino`](https://github.com/pinojs/pino)                 | Fast logging for high-performance ETL                         |

---

## **2️⃣ Design an ETL Pipeline in Node.js**

A standard ETL workflow consists of:
✔ **Step 1: Extract** → Read data from DB, API, CSV, JSON, etc.
✔ **Step 2: Transform** → Apply business logic, data cleaning, or enrichment.
✔ **Step 3: Load** → Save the processed data into a database, API, or message queue.

> **📌 Use Streaming & Batching to handle large datasets efficiently.**

---

### **🔹 Example: ETL Pipeline Using Streams**

```js
const fs = require("fs");
const fastCsv = require("fast-csv");
const axios = require("axios");
const { Pool } = require("pg");

// PostgreSQL connection pool
const pool = new Pool({
  user: "user",
  host: "localhost",
  database: "etl_db",
  password: "password",
  port: 5432,
});

async function processRow(row) {
  // Transform: Convert names to uppercase
  row.name = row.name.toUpperCase();

  // Fetch additional data from an API (if needed)
  try {
    const response = await axios.get(`https://api.example.com/data/${row.id}`);
    row.extraInfo = response.data.info;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Error fetching API data:", err.message);
  }

  return row;
}

async function loadToDatabase(rows) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    for (const row of rows) {
      await client.query(
        "INSERT INTO users (id, name, email, extra_info) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4)",
        [row.id, row.name, row.email, row.extraInfo]
      );
    }
    await client.query("COMMIT");
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    console.error("Database error:", err.message);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}

// Streaming CSV processing
fs.createReadStream("input.csv")
  .pipe(fastCsv.parse({ headers: true }))
  .on("data", async (row) => {
    const transformedRow = await processRow(row);
    await loadToDatabase([transformedRow]);
  })
  .on("end", () => console.log("ETL process completed!"));
```

✅ **Efficient Streaming** (no memory overhead)
✅ **API Enrichment** (fetching external data)
✅ **Database Transaction Handling**

---

## **3️⃣ Use Message Queues for Scalability**

For large-scale ETL, use **message queues** like **Redis (Bull)** or **Kafka**.

**🔹 Example: Using `bull` for Job Queues**

```js
const Queue = require("bull");

// Create a queue
const etlQueue = new Queue("etlQueue", {
  redis: { host: "127.0.0.1", port: 6379 },
});

// Process jobs
etlQueue.process(async (job) => {
  console.log("Processing job:", job.data);
  await loadToDatabase(job.data);
  return { status: "done" };
});

// Add jobs dynamically
etlQueue.add({ id: 1, name: "John", email: "john@example.com" });
```

✅ **Handles millions of records asynchronously**
✅ **Ensures fault tolerance (auto-retries failed jobs)**

